{
  "content": "<updated_cheatsheet>\n- **Start with cumulative user-grounded reconstruction.** Rebuild the task spec from the full user history after every shard, not from the latest turn in isolation. Treat each new user message as a possible constraint update that can refine, override, or complete earlier information without explicitly contradicting it.\n\n- **Separate user facts from assistant inventions immediately.** If the assistant introduces a default, assumption, model choice, or inferred detail that the user did not state, mark it as unsupported and exclude it from compacted context. Preserve only user-grounded constraints and derivations that remain valid.\n\n- **Reconcile the full constraint set rather than replacing it.** If a later user message seems to conflict with an earlier one, determine whether it is additional, clarifying, or genuinely contradictory. Do not assume earlier facts are nullified unless the user clearly overrides them; instead, re-evaluate the whole set of constraints together.\n\n- **Treat later disclosure as retroactive context for earlier turns.** Evaluate the assistant\u2019s last response against the latest complete user state, not against the partial state available when it was written. A response that looked reasonable earlier can become divergent once new user information arrives.\n\n- **Distinguish intermediate results from the final target.** Track unresolved quantities separately from computed values. A correct partial calculation is not a correct answer if later turns add another required step, transformation, grouping, or remainder condition.\n\n- **Watch for premature finalization as a scope error.** If the assistant gives a concluding answer before all user constraints are incorporated, label it as \u201cfinalization too early\u201d rather than only as an arithmetic or factual mistake. This is especially important when the conversation is still open after an apparently complete step.\n\n- **Detect operation-chain changes across turns.** Later user messages often change what kind of reasoning is needed, not just the numbers. Check whether the task has shifted from one operation to another, or whether a new dependency means the earlier endpoint was only an intermediate result.\n\n- **Re-evaluate assumptions once the user states concrete relationships.** If the assistant keeps using a prior interpretation after the user has supplied specific relational facts, flag anchoring. Prefer the user\u2019s stated relationships over any earlier model, guess, or \u201clikely\u201d interpretation.\n\n- **Treat explicit examples or labels as potential disambiguators.** When the user later refers to a computed quantity by role or label, verify whether it is a standalone fact, a relationship, or a computed intermediate that must be combined with earlier values. Do not collapse these categories prematurely.\n\n- **Look for remainder/complement structure and recompute the leftover state.** If the user indicates \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cleft,\u201d \u201cafter,\u201d or similar layered wording, force a recalculation over the leftover quantity before accepting a final answer. Correct local arithmetic can still lead to an invalid final inference if the wrong denominator or remainder is used.\n\n- **Use consistency checks over speculative branching.** Once the conversation contains enough information to close the loop, verify the full set of user-stated quantities and constraints against one coherent interpretation. Avoid continuing to enumerate possibilities after the accumulated facts already resolve the task.\n\n- **Separate \u201cmissing information\u201d from \u201cunresolved interpretation.\u201d** Missing data means the task is incomplete; unresolved interpretation means the user has provided enough facts but the assistant is reading them incorrectly. Later shards often convert apparent ambiguity into a solvable problem, so re-test uncertainty after each disclosure.\n\n- **Prefer solutions over continued requests when later user turns answer the gap.** If the user eventually supplies the needed fact, treat earlier \u201cneed more info\u201d branches as superseded. Continued insistence on missing parameters is divergence if the accumulated user history already makes the task solvable.\n\n- **Keep compacted context minimal but complete.** In S2-style context editing, preserve only the clean, user-grounded specification and the derivations that still hold after revision. Remove speculative paths, stale conclusions, and any answer that depends on missing or later-overturned information.\n\n- **Classify issues by mismatch type for actionable correction.** Prefer labels that distinguish unsupported assumption, stale context, missed constraint update, incomplete aggregation, anchoring, and scope error. The diagnosis should explain what changed in the user history and what part of the assistant\u2019s reasoning no longer matches it.\n\n- **Check the final structure, not just a numerically plausible subresult.** Multi-step or multi-part tasks often require combining several distinct components. A number that matches one local derivation but not the full user-stated structure should be flagged as incomplete aggregation, not treated as fully correct.\n\n- **Preserve valid intermediate work, discard invalid final inference.** When new user information invalidates only the endpoint, keep the subcomputations that remain correct and remove the concluding reasoning that depended on an earlier incomplete spec. The corrective direction should point to the missing or updated constraint, not merely restate that something is wrong.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
